<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Michel Colombe</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1430–c. 1513</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Michel Colombe was a French sculptor from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a>, the leading sculptor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> at the beginning of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>. His best-known work is the tomb of Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-ii">Francis II</a> of Brittany and Marguerite of Foix, made in 1502–1507 for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anne-of-brittany">Anne of Brittany</a> and now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nantes-cathedral">Nantes Cathedral</a>. Its figures of the Virtues blend late Gothic and Italian influences.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/michel-colombe.jpg" alt="Michel Colombe" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Photo prise par Jibi44 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tomb_of_Francis_II,_Duke_of_Brittany.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
